impetus.options.pages.memory=Memory
impetus.options.coarctatio.resource_locations.name=Deduplicate Resource Names
impetus.options.coarctatio.resource_locations.tooltip=Shares the namespace and path strings behind every ResourceLocation. A modded instance holds hundreds of thousands of these, and the namespace is one of a few hundred mod IDs repeated over and over.\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.coarctatio.model_variants.name=Deduplicate Model Variants
impetus.options.coarctatio.model_variants.tooltip=Shares the variant string of every ModelResourceLocation. "normal" and "inventory" alone account for most of them.\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.coarctatio.quad_vertex_data.name=Pool Quad Vertex Data
impetus.options.coarctatio.quad_vertex_data.tooltip=Every block face bakes to a 28-integer vertex array, and identical geometry produces byte-identical arrays. Pooling them collapses millions down to tens of thousands.\n\nQuads whose vertex data may be rewritten later are never pooled, so this cannot corrupt rendering.\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.coarctatio.multipart_conditions.name=Flatten Multipart Conditions
impetus.options.coarctatio.multipart_conditions.tooltip=Multipart blockstates (fences, walls, panes, wires, pipes) compile to a tree of predicate objects, one set per block. This flattens each tree into a single object holding arrays, then shares identical ones between blocks.\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.coarctatio.block_states.name=Compress Block States
impetus.options.coarctatio.block_states.tooltip=The single largest saving available. Vanilla gives every block state its own lookup table mapping each property value to the resulting state — hundreds of megabytes of pure redundancy in a large pack.\n\nThis replaces the table with one packed integer per state and one shared array per block. Any block the packer cannot handle silently keeps vanilla states.\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.coarctatio.state_properties.name=Compact State Property Maps
impetus.options.coarctatio.state_properties.tooltip=Shares one property-key array across every state of a block, so each state stores only its values.\n\nRequires Compress Block States, and a JVM that permits the necessary class injection. Falls back to the standard map if either is unavailable.\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.coarctatio.nbt_backing_map.name=Compact NBT Storage
impetus.options.coarctatio.nbt_backing_map.tooltip=NBT is the most numerous object graph in a modded save: every item stack, tile entity, entity and packet. Vanilla gives each compound a full hash map even though most hold four or five entries.\n\nThis uses flat array storage for small compounds and promotes to a hash map only when one grows.\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.coarctatio.nbt_keys.name=Share NBT Keys
impetus.options.coarctatio.nbt_keys.tooltip=NBT keys are drawn from a tiny shared vocabulary — id, Count, Damage, tag, x, y, z — but every compound normally holds its own copy of each string. This shares them.\n\nApplies to compounds created from now on.
impetus.options.coarctatio.nbt_threshold.name=NBT Array Storage Limit
impetus.options.coarctatio.nbt_threshold.tooltip=Entry count at which an NBT compound switches from flat array storage to a hash map. Array storage is smaller and faster to search at small sizes; a hash map wins once a compound grows past this.\n\nSet to 0 to always use hash maps.\n\nApplies to compounds created from now on.
impetus.options.coarctatio.baked_models.name=Compact Baked Models
impetus.options.coarctatio.baked_models.tooltip=Replaces the growable lists and maps inside baked models with exactly-sized immutable ones, removing the unused growth capacity every model carries.\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.coarctatio.model_graph.name=Compact Model Registry
impetus.options.coarctatio.model_graph.tooltip=Swaps the model loader's hash maps for a more compact implementation. The largest holds one entry per blockstate variant — tens of thousands in a large pack.\n\nMaps whose ordering affects which model wins a collision are left alone.\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.coarctatio.chunk_nbt.name=Strip Loaded Chunk Data
impetus.options.coarctatio.chunk_nbt.tooltip=Chunks are loaded in two stages, and the entire chunk tag — tens of kilobytes of block and light arrays — stays in memory between them even though it has already been copied out.\n\nThis drops everything the second stage does not read.\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.coarctatio.empty_sections.name=Drop Empty Chunk Sections
impetus.options.coarctatio.empty_sections.tooltip=A chunk section whose blocks have all been mined out still loads a full block-state container and light arrays, roughly ten kilobytes for pure air.\n\nSections are only dropped when their lighting can be recomputed exactly, so this cannot darken caves or overhangs.\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.coarctatio.debug_overlay.name=Show Memory Statistics
impetus.options.coarctatio.debug_overlay.tooltip=Adds a Coarctatio line to the F3 debug overlay showing how much has been pooled.
impetus.options.coarctatio.class_loader_cache.name=Reclaim Class Loader Cache
impetus.options.coarctatio.class_loader_cache.tooltip=The mod loader keeps the raw bytecode of every class it has ever loaded for the entire session, which in a large pack is hundreds of megabytes doing nothing after startup.\n\nThis lets the garbage collector reclaim it while still serving anything that asks later.\n\nUsually the single largest saving available.\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.coarctatio.sprite_data.name=Release Texture Pixel Data
impetus.options.coarctatio.sprite_data.tooltip=Every stitched texture keeps a full copy of its pixels in memory after the atlas has been uploaded to the graphics card. This releases them.\n\nAnimated textures are left alone, since they need their frames every tick.\n\nDisable this if a texture mod misbehaves.\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.coarctatio.model_transforms.name=Share Model Transforms
impetus.options.coarctatio.model_transforms.tooltip=Every baked model carries a set of camera transforms and an item override list, and the overwhelming majority of them are identical. This shares one copy between all of them.\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.coarctatio.bake_state.name=Release Model Build Data
impetus.options.coarctatio.bake_state.tooltip=Frees the unbaked models and load errors the model loader keeps after baking is finished. Released only once every mod has had its chance to read them.\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.coarctatio.runtime_collections.name=Compact Runtime Collections
impetus.options.coarctatio.runtime_collections.tooltip=Replaces the hash maps behind game registries, entity synced data and the per-chunk entity lookup with more compact implementations.\n\nThe entity ones scale with how much is loaded rather than with any setting.\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.coarctatio.search_trees.name=Lazy Creative Search Index
impetus.options.coarctatio.search_trees.tooltip=The creative search box builds a full text index over every item in the game at startup and on every resource reload, whether or not you ever search.\n\nThis defers it until the first search. With a recipe-viewer mod installed it is never built at all, since those replace the search UI entirely.\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.coarctatio.clear_on_leave.name=Free Pools On Leaving World
impetus.options.coarctatio.clear_on_leave.tooltip=Two pools grow while you play rather than while loading: NBT keys, and resource paths from things like downloaded skins. Neither shrinks on its own.\n\nThis frees them when you return to the main menu, so a long session does not carry its strings into the next world.\n\nApplies immediately.

impetus.options.pages.lighting=Lighting
impetus.options.fulgor.enabled.name=Enable Fulgor
impetus.options.fulgor.enabled.tooltip=Master switch for Impetus' lighting engine. Turning this off restores vanilla lighting entirely, which is the first thing to try if you suspect a lighting bug.\n\nAutomatically disabled when Cubic Chunks is installed, since that mod brings its own engine.\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.fulgor.deferred_updates.name=Batch Light Updates
impetus.options.fulgor.deferred_updates.tooltip=Vanilla propagates light the instant a block changes, re-lighting the same positions once for every path that reaches them. This records the change instead and resolves the whole batch the next time something actually reads light.\n\nThe single biggest win available: a large build, an explosion or a chunk of world generation stops paying for every intermediate lighting state on the way to the final one.\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.fulgor.deduplicate_updates.name=Collapse Duplicate Updates
impetus.options.fulgor.deduplicate_updates.tooltip=A bulk change schedules the same block from every neighbour that touched it. This drops the repeats so each position is evaluated once per batch.\n\nUse /fulgor to see what share of scheduled updates is being collapsed; under heavy building it is usually most of them.\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.fulgor.block_light_info.name=Cache Block Light Properties
impetus.options.fulgor.block_light_info.tooltip=Most blocks emit and block a fixed amount of light no matter where they are, but asking them costs several virtual calls that a large modpack makes impossible to optimise.\n\nThis works out once per block whether its answer can vary, and takes the short path when it cannot.\n\nDisabled automatically when Dynamic Lights or Fluidlogged API is installed, since both make light depend on the position.\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.fulgor.parallel_updates.name=Parallel Light Updates
impetus.options.fulgor.parallel_updates.tooltip=Splits a large batch of pending light updates by chunk and runs the chunks across a pool of threads, after ScalableLux's parallel Starlight.\n\nA pass reaches at most fifteen blocks from a scheduled position, so chunks four or more apart never see each other's writes and run at once; closer ones wait their turn. Each pass settles its region to the same fixpoint the single pass would, so the lighting is identical, only sooner. Pays off when many chunks light at once: generation, a fast flight, a large explosion. Small batches still run on the calling thread.\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.fulgor.chunk_boundaries.name=Fix Chunk Boundary Lighting
impetus.options.fulgor.chunk_boundaries.tooltip=Vanilla drops a light update that would cross into a chunk which is not loaded, and never revisits it. That is where the light seams along chunk borders in newly generated terrain come from.\n\nThis records what was skipped and replays it when the neighbour arrives.\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.fulgor.section_light.name=Send Carved-Out Section Light
impetus.options.fulgor.section_light.tooltip=A chunk section with no blocks left in it is never sent to the client, which then assumes full daylight. Inside a large cave or a hollowed-out mountain that is badly wrong.\n\nThis sends such sections when their lighting is not what the client would have assumed.\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.fulgor.render_updates.name=Optimise Render Light Updates
impetus.options.fulgor.render_updates.tooltip=Vanilla only turns light changes into chunk rebuilds when the chunk builder happens to be idle, so under load they can go unrendered indefinitely.\n\nThis drains them every tick instead, through a compact queue that collapses repeats.\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.fulgor.skip_while_paused.name=Pause Light Updates
impetus.options.fulgor.skip_while_paused.tooltip=Stops processing light while the game is paused. Nothing can change in that state, so the work would be wasted.\n\nApplies immediately.
impetus.options.fulgor.debug_overlay.name=Show Lighting Statistics
impetus.options.fulgor.debug_overlay.tooltip=Adds a Fulgor line to the F3 debug overlay showing how many light updates have been scheduled and what share was collapsed.
impetus.options.fulgor.thread_warnings.name=Warn On Off-Thread Access
impetus.options.fulgor.thread_warnings.tooltip=Logs a warning, with a stack trace, when a mod changes a world's lighting from a thread that does not own it. Fulgor handles the violation either way, but it can cause stalls and it is always worth reporting.\n\nApplies immediately.

impetus.options.pages.optimizations=Optimizations

impetus.options.equilibrium.ai.name=Mob AI
impetus.options.equilibrium.ai.tooltip=Mob AI optimizations\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.equilibrium.ai_goal_selector.name=Goal Selector
impetus.options.equilibrium.ai_goal_selector.tooltip=The AI goal selector holds its task sets in fastutil linked open hash sets rather than java.util.LinkedHashSet. Both sets are walked every tick for every mob in range, and the vanilla ones put every task behind its own node.\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.equilibrium.alloc.name=Allocations
impetus.options.equilibrium.alloc.tooltip=Patches that reduce memory allocations\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.equilibrium.alloc_entity_tracker.name=Entity Tracker
impetus.options.equilibrium.alloc_entity_tracker.tooltip=Entity trackers store their players in a fastutil open hash set instead of a java.util.HashSet, which allocates a node per player per tracked entity.\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.equilibrium.alloc_deep_passengers.name=Deep Passengers
impetus.options.equilibrium.alloc_deep_passengers.tooltip=Collecting an entity's passengers stops allocating a hash map for the overwhelmingly common case of an entity carrying nobody, and uses an identity set when it does carry someone.\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.equilibrium.alloc_enum_values.name=Enum Values
impetus.options.equilibrium.alloc_enum_values.tooltip=Avoid Enum#values() array copies in frequently called code\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.equilibrium.alloc_enum_values_piston_block.name=Piston Block
impetus.options.equilibrium.alloc_enum_values_piston_block.tooltip=Piston extension checks reuse the shared facing array instead of copying EnumFacing.values().\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.equilibrium.alloc_enum_values_piston_handler.name=Piston Handler
impetus.options.equilibrium.alloc_enum_values_piston_handler.tooltip=The piston structure resolver reuses the shared facing array instead of copying EnumFacing.values() once per block it moves.\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.equilibrium.alloc_enum_values_redstone_wire.name=Redstone Wire
impetus.options.equilibrium.alloc_enum_values_redstone_wire.tooltip=Redstone wire power propagation reuses the shared facing array instead of copying EnumFacing.values() once per wire per power level.\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.equilibrium.block.name=Blocks
impetus.options.equilibrium.block.tooltip=Optimizations related to blocks\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.equilibrium.block_hopper.name=Hopper
impetus.options.equilibrium.block_hopper.tooltip=Hoppers remember the inventory above them and the one they face, instead of searching the world for both on every transfer attempt. An idle hopper attempts a transfer every tick, and each attempt that finds nothing costs a full entity query over the block.\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.equilibrium.block_redstone_wire.name=Redstone Wire
impetus.options.equilibrium.block_redstone_wire.tooltip=Redstone wire power calculation reads each neighbouring block once instead of twice, and resolves the block above the wire once per update rather than once per direction.\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.equilibrium.chunk.name=Chunks
impetus.options.equilibrium.chunk.tooltip=Various world chunk optimizations\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.equilibrium.chunk_no_validation.name=No Validation
impetus.options.equilibrium.chunk_no_validation.tooltip=Block reads no longer ask the world what type it is on every access in order to find out whether it is the debug world. The answer is fixed when the world is constructed, so it is resolved once.\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.equilibrium.collections.name=Collections
impetus.options.equilibrium.collections.tooltip=Various collection optimizations\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.equilibrium.collections_mob_spawning.name=Mob Spawning
impetus.options.equilibrium.collections_mob_spawning.tooltip=The set of chunks eligible for mob spawning is a fastutil open hash set rather than a java.util.HashSet. It is rebuilt every spawn cycle and probed once per candidate chunk per player.\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.equilibrium.entity.name=Entities
impetus.options.equilibrium.entity.tooltip=Various entity optimizations\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.equilibrium.entity_collisions.name=Collisions
impetus.options.equilibrium.entity_collisions.tooltip=Various entity collision optimizations\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.equilibrium.entity_collisions_movement.name=Movement
impetus.options.equilibrium.entity_collisions_movement.tooltip=Gathering the blocks an entity could collide with resolves a chunk section once per column rather than once per block. The search walks a column at a time, so sixteen consecutive reads share one section.\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.equilibrium.entity_fast_elytra_check.name=Fast Elytra Check
impetus.options.equilibrium.entity_fast_elytra_check.tooltip=Skip writing to the tracked-data table every tick to record that a living entity is still not elytra flying. The write was already a no-op; the lookup it needed was not.\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.equilibrium.entity_fast_retrieval.name=Fast Retrieval
impetus.options.equilibrium.entity_fast_retrieval.tooltip=Entity queries resolve each chunk once instead of testing whether it is loaded and then fetching the chunk that test just found.\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.equilibrium.entity_fast_hand_swing.name=Fast Hand Swing
impetus.options.equilibrium.entity_fast_hand_swing.tooltip=Skip the hand swing progress and animation maths when the hand is not swinging. The division vanilla performs there needs the swing duration, and working that out means checking the entity's haste and mining fatigue effects.\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.equilibrium.math.name=Maths
impetus.options.equilibrium.math.tooltip=Various math optimizations\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.equilibrium.math_fast_blockpos.name=Fast Blockpos
impetus.options.equilibrium.math_fast_blockpos.tooltip=Directional block position offsets are computed inline, and each direction's offsets are stored rather than derived from its axis on every read.\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.equilibrium.math_fast_util.name=Fast Util
impetus.options.equilibrium.math_fast_util.tooltip=Direction opposites avoid a modulo, and picking a random direction stops cloning the values array twice per call.\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.equilibrium.math_sine_lut.name=Sine LUT
impetus.options.equilibrium.math_sine_lut.tooltip=Replaces the 256 KB sine table with a 64 KB one that fits in cache, using trigonometric identities to reconstruct the quadrants it drops. Results are bit-for-bit identical to vanilla, and verified to be at startup.\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.equilibrium.util.name=Internals
impetus.options.equilibrium.util.tooltip=Various utilities for other mixins. These add no optimization on their own; turning one off disables everything that depends on it.\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.equilibrium.util_block_entity_retrieval.name=Block Entity Retrieval
impetus.options.equilibrium.util_block_entity_retrieval.tooltip=Allows looking up a tile entity that already exists without constructing and registering one as a side effect of asking.\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.equilibrium.util_data_storage.name=Data Storage
impetus.options.equilibrium.util_data_storage.tooltip=Stores Equilibrium's per-world scratch data. None of it is saved; it exists so the other optimizations have somewhere to keep their counters.\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.equilibrium.util_chunk_access.name=Chunk Access
impetus.options.equilibrium.util_chunk_access.tooltip=Access a world's loaded chunks directly, without going through the chunk provider's dispatch and its map lookup.\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.equilibrium.world.name=World
impetus.options.equilibrium.world.tooltip=Various world related optimizations\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.equilibrium.world_block_entity_ticking.name=Block Entity Ticking
impetus.options.equilibrium.world_block_entity_ticking.tooltip=Various tile entity ticking optimizations\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.equilibrium.world_block_entity_ticking_sleeping.name=Sleeping
impetus.options.equilibrium.world_block_entity_ticking_sleeping.tooltip=Allows tile entities to skip work they can prove will do nothing.\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.equilibrium.world_block_entity_ticking_sleeping_brewing_stand.name=Brewing Stand
impetus.options.equilibrium.world_block_entity_ticking_sleeping_brewing_stand.tooltip=A brewing stand with no ingredient stops asking the brewing registry whether it can brew. On Forge that question walks every recipe every mod has registered, and it is asked once per stand per tick.\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.equilibrium.world_block_entity_ticking_sleeping_furnace.name=Furnace
impetus.options.equilibrium.world_block_entity_ticking_sleeping_furnace.tooltip=A furnace that is unlit, has nothing part-cooked and is missing either its fuel or its input skips its tick entirely. Every branch it would have taken is a no-op.\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.equilibrium.world_explosions.name=Explosions
impetus.options.equilibrium.world_explosions.tooltip=Various improvements to explosions.\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.equilibrium.world_explosions_block_raycast.name=Block Raycast
impetus.options.equilibrium.world_explosions_block_raycast.tooltip=Explosion block damage walks its 1352 rays through a chunk-section cursor and reads each position at most once, instead of a fresh world lookup and a fresh block position for every step of every ray.\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.equilibrium.world_explosions_entity_raycast.name=Entity Raycast
impetus.options.equilibrium.world_explosions_entity_raycast.tooltip=Explosion entity exposure caches the blocks its sample rays cross. Every ray of the same explosion crosses mostly the same blocks, and vanilla re-traces all of them for every entity.\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.equilibrium.world_inline_block_access.name=Inline Block Access
impetus.options.equilibrium.world_inline_block_access.tooltip=Block and block-state reads resolve the chunk through a small direct cache rather than the chunk provider's map lookup on every access.\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.equilibrium.world_inline_height.name=Inline Height
impetus.options.equilibrium.world_inline_height.tooltip=World height queries resolve the chunk once instead of testing whether it is loaded and then fetching the chunk that test just found.\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.equilibrium.world_raycast.name=Raycast
impetus.options.equilibrium.world_raycast.tooltip=Block ray tracing steps through the world without allocating a vector and a block position per step.\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.equilibrium.world_tick_scheduler.name=Tick Scheduler
impetus.options.equilibrium.world_tick_scheduler.tooltip=The set of pending scheduled block ticks is a fastutil open hash set rather than a java.util.HashSet, which removes a node allocation per scheduled tick and a pointer chase from every lookup. Redstone, liquids and crops schedule thousands of these per second on a busy world.\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.equilibrium.worldgen.name=World Generation
impetus.options.equilibrium.worldgen.tooltip=World generation optimizations, after Noisium's approach of bypassing the abstractions between the generator and the storage it fills. Every rule here keeps generation bit-for-bit identical to vanilla.\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.equilibrium.worldgen_primer_state_cache.name=Primer State Cache
impetus.options.equilibrium.worldgen_primer_state_cache.tooltip=The chunk primer remembers the last state it encoded and the last id it decoded. Terrain writes long runs of stone and water, and surface replacement reads columns back one block at a time, so most of the identity-map lookups per block become a reference compare.\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.equilibrium.worldgen_chunk_copy.name=Direct Chunk Fill
impetus.options.equilibrium.worldgen_chunk_copy.tooltip=Copying a finished primer into a chunk no longer reads back the slot it is about to write. Every slot of a new section starts as air, so the block and tick counters are updated directly and the state goes straight into the palette storage.\n\nTakes effect after a restart.
impetus.options.equilibrium.worldgen_int_cache.name=Per-Thread Layer Scratch
impetus.options.equilibrium.worldgen_int_cache.tooltip=Pools the biome layer scratch arrays per thread instead of in one locked global pool. Vanilla's pool hands every in-use array back on reset, so two threads walking the layers at once (a parallel spawn pass and a chunk generating, or two dimensions) would reclaim each other's buffers mid-use.\n\nTakes effect after a restart.

