# Extras
#
impetus.options.pages.extras=Extras

impetus.options.extras.overlay.fps=FPS: %s
impetus.options.extras.overlay.fps_extended=(avg %s, 1%% low %s, 0.1%% low %s)
impetus.options.extras.overlay.coordinates=XYZ: %s / %s / %s
impetus.options.extras.overlay.light_updates=Light updates are disabled
impetus.options.extras.overlay.budget=Budget %s: %sms / %sms target, pressure %s%%%s
impetus.options.extras.overlay.budget.adaptive= (adaptive)
impetus.options.extras.overlay.budget.off=Render budget: off
impetus.options.extras.overlay.budget.limits=Particles %s%% | Mobs %s | Blocks %s
impetus.options.extras.overlay.budget.distance_idle=%sm (idle)
impetus.options.extras.overlay.budget.distance_armed=%sm
impetus.options.extras.overlay.budget.counters=Skipped/kept: mobs %s/%s, particles %s/%s, blocks %s/%s, frames %s/%s

impetus.options.extras.animations.all.name=Animations
impetus.options.extras.animations.all.tooltip=Master switch for animated block textures.\n\nAn animated texture is re-uploaded to the atlas every time it advances a frame, whether or not any of it is on screen. Turning the lot off is one of the larger single wins available on a weak GPU, at the cost of every fluid, fire and portal in the world being frozen on one frame.
impetus.options.extras.animations.water.name=Water
impetus.options.extras.animations.water.tooltip=Animates water textures. Still and flowing water are the most-uploaded animated textures in an ordinary world simply because there is so much of it.
impetus.options.extras.animations.lava.name=Lava
impetus.options.extras.animations.lava.tooltip=Animates lava textures.
impetus.options.extras.animations.fire.name=Fire
impetus.options.extras.animations.fire.tooltip=Animates fire textures.
impetus.options.extras.animations.portal.name=Nether Portal
impetus.options.extras.animations.portal.tooltip=Animates the nether portal texture.
impetus.options.extras.animations.redstone.name=Redstone
impetus.options.extras.animations.redstone.tooltip=Animates redstone textures, and allows redstone dust particles to spawn.
impetus.options.extras.animations.explosion.name=Explosions
impetus.options.extras.animations.explosion.tooltip=Animates explosion textures, and allows explosion particles to spawn.
impetus.options.extras.animations.flame.name=Flames
impetus.options.extras.animations.flame.tooltip=Animates flame textures, and allows flame particles to spawn.
impetus.options.extras.animations.smoke.name=Smoke
impetus.options.extras.animations.smoke.tooltip=Animates smoke textures, and allows smoke particles to spawn.
impetus.options.extras.animations.sculk_sensor.name=Sculk Sensors
impetus.options.extras.animations.sculk_sensor.tooltip=Animates sculk sensor textures.\n\n1.12.2 has no sculk sensor; this exists for mods that add one and name its texture accordingly.
impetus.options.extras.animations.block.name=Other Blocks
impetus.options.extras.animations.block.tooltip=Animates every other block texture, vanilla and modded — sea lanterns, magma, prismarine, and anything a mod animates.

impetus.options.extras.particles.all.name=Particles
impetus.options.extras.particles.all.tooltip=Master switch for all particles.\n\nParticles are ticked and re-sorted individually on the client thread, so a dense effect costs CPU time well before it costs GPU time. This is the switch to reach for when a modded machine or a mob farm tanks the frame rate.
impetus.options.extras.particles.rain_splash.name=Rain Splashes
impetus.options.extras.particles.rain_splash.tooltip=Spawns splash particles where rain lands.\n\nDisabling this skips the search for landing spots, not just the particles: the game raycasts candidate positions around the player every tick while it is raining.
impetus.options.extras.particles.block_break.name=Block Break
impetus.options.extras.particles.block_break.tooltip=Spawns the burst of particles when a block is destroyed.
impetus.options.extras.particles.block_breaking.name=Block Breaking
impetus.options.extras.particles.block_breaking.tooltip=Spawns the trickle of particles while a block is being mined.
impetus.options.extras.particles.void.name=Void Particles
impetus.options.extras.particles.void.tooltip=Spawns the drifting particles seen near the bottom of the world.
impetus.options.extras.particles.water.name=Water Particles
impetus.options.extras.particles.water.tooltip=Spawns the suspended particles seen underwater.
impetus.options.extras.particles.portal.name=Portal Particles
impetus.options.extras.particles.portal.tooltip=Spawns nether portal and end gateway particles.
impetus.options.extras.particles.potion.name=Potion Particles
impetus.options.extras.particles.potion.tooltip=Spawns the swirls around an entity under a potion effect.\n\nWorth knowing before you turn this off: those swirls are how you notice you have been poisoned or given a beneficial effect.
impetus.options.extras.particles.dripping.name=Dripping Water & Lava
impetus.options.extras.particles.dripping.tooltip=Spawns the drips that fall from water and lava overhead.
impetus.options.extras.particles.firework.name=Firework Particles
impetus.options.extras.particles.firework.tooltip=Spawns firework sparks. A large rocket volley is one of the densest particle effects in the game.
impetus.options.extras.particles.parallel.name=Parallel Ticking
impetus.options.extras.particles.parallel.tooltip=Ticks particles across a small pool of worker threads instead of the client thread, after Harvey_Husky's AsyncParticles.\n\nThe client thread waits for the batch, so nothing else touches the world meanwhile; the only shared table, the spawn queue, is made concurrent. Layers under 256 particles keep vanilla's loop since the hand-off would cost more than it saves. Worth the most in the scenes the particle budget also targets: mob farms, machines and weather.
impetus.options.extras.particles.parallel_modded.name=Modded Particles
impetus.options.extras.particles.parallel_modded.tooltip=Also ticks particle classes from other mods on the pool. Off (the default) keeps them on the client thread, since a modded particle may write to a shared list or map during its tick. Vanilla particles are unaffected either way.
impetus.options.extras.particles.light_cache.name=Light Cache
impetus.options.extras.particles.light_cache.tooltip=Samples each particle's light once when it ticks rather than once every frame it is drawn. Vanilla walks the chunk's light arrays for every particle on every frame, so at high frame rates this is most of what the render pass spends; the sample is at most one tick old. Dynamic lights are still applied at draw time.
impetus.options.extras.particles.cull_offscreen.name=Cull Off-Screen Particles
impetus.options.extras.particles.cull_offscreen.tooltip=Tests each vanilla particle against the view frustum before its quad is built, so particles behind the camera or off to the side cost nothing to draw. Modded particles are always drawn, since one may render outside the box it reports.
impetus.options.extras.particles.collision_cache.name=Collision Cache
impetus.options.extras.particles.collision_cache.tooltip=Particle Core's open-air shortcut. Every colliding particle sweeps the blocks around it each tick; a particle drifting inside a cell that has nothing to collide with keeps that answer until it leaves the cell (or every ten ticks), so smoke, flames and drips in open air skip the sweep almost entirely.

impetus.options.extras.details.sky.name=Sky
impetus.options.extras.details.sky.tooltip=Draws the sky box.\n\nThis takes the sun, moon and stars with it — vanilla draws them all in the same pass. Use the switches below to keep the sky while dropping what is on it.
impetus.options.extras.details.stars.name=Stars
impetus.options.extras.details.stars.tooltip=Draws the star field at night.
impetus.options.extras.details.total_stars.name=Star Count
impetus.options.extras.details.total_stars.tooltip=How many stars to generate. Vanilla uses 1500.\n\nThe star field is built once into a buffer, so this costs geometry rather than frame time — but it is rebuilt on a renderer reload, which is why changing it triggers one.
impetus.options.extras.details.sun.name=Sun
impetus.options.extras.details.sun.tooltip=Draws the sun disc.
impetus.options.extras.details.moon.name=Moon
impetus.options.extras.details.moon.tooltip=Draws the moon disc.
impetus.options.extras.details.rain_snow.name=Rain & Snow
impetus.options.extras.details.rain_snow.tooltip=Draws falling rain and snow.\n\nOnly the drawing. The weather still happens, so mob spawning, crop growth and sounds are unaffected — use the Weather option below to change the weather itself.
impetus.options.extras.details.biome_colors.name=Biome Colors
impetus.options.extras.details.biome_colors.tooltip=Tints grass, foliage and water by biome.\n\nThe cost is not the lookup but the blend around it: with a blend radius of 7 the game averages 225 biome samples for every block face it colours. Turning this off skips all of it and makes every biome look like plains.
impetus.options.extras.details.sky_colors.name=Sky Colors
impetus.options.extras.details.sky_colors.tooltip=Varies the sky colour by biome.\n\nOff gives every biome the same overworld blue while keeping vanilla's time-of-day and weather shading.
impetus.options.extras.details.swamp_colors.name=Swamp Colors
impetus.options.extras.details.swamp_colors.tooltip=Uses the swamp's own grey-green grass and foliage tint.\n\nOff gives swamps the ordinary temperature-and-rainfall colour every other biome uses.
impetus.options.extras.details.void_fog.name=Void Fog
impetus.options.extras.details.void_fog.tooltip=Darkens the view near the bottom of the world.
impetus.options.extras.details.capes.name=Capes
impetus.options.extras.details.capes.tooltip=Draws player capes. Elytra are unaffected.
impetus.options.extras.details.held_item_tooltips.name=Held Item Tooltips
impetus.options.extras.details.held_item_tooltips.tooltip=Shows the item name above the hotbar when you change slots.

impetus.options.extras.render.fog.name=Fog
impetus.options.extras.render.fog.tooltip=Draws atmospheric fog.\n\nFog that conveys gameplay state — blindness, being underwater, being in lava — is never removed by this, whatever it is set to.
impetus.options.extras.render.fog_start.name=Fog Start
impetus.options.extras.render.fog_start.tooltip=Where fog begins, as a percentage of where it ends. Lower values start the haze closer to the camera.
impetus.options.extras.render.fog_distance.name=Fog Distance
impetus.options.extras.render.fog_distance.tooltip=How far away fog becomes fully opaque, in chunks. Default follows the render distance.\n\nThis is not purely cosmetic: Impetus stops walking the chunk graph past the point fog is opaque, so a shorter fog distance means fewer chunks considered as well as fewer drawn.
impetus.options.extras.render.fog_shape.name=Fog Shape
impetus.options.extras.render.fog_shape.tooltip=Which distance the terrain shader fogs by.\n\nOnly terrain goes through that shader — entities, particles and the sky still fog by distance from the eye. Anything but Vanilla therefore makes terrain and what is drawn over it disagree about where fog starts, which is the point: it is how you fog the horizon without fogging the sky.
impetus.options.extras.fog_shape.vanilla=Vanilla
impetus.options.extras.fog_shape.cylindrical=Cylindrical
impetus.options.extras.fog_shape.radial=Radial
impetus.options.extras.fog_shape.planar=Planar

impetus.options.extras.render.cloud_scale.name=Cloud Scale
impetus.options.extras.render.cloud_scale.tooltip=Size of each cloud cell, relative to vanilla.\n\nLarger cells cover the same sky with fewer of them, so this is cheaper as well as different. Cloud height and distance live on the Quality page.
impetus.options.extras.render.cloud_translucency.name=Cloud Translucency
impetus.options.extras.render.cloud_translucency.tooltip=When clouds turn translucent.\n\nVanilla compares your altitude against a hard-coded 128, which is wrong as soon as the cloud height changes at all. Default uses the dimension's actual cloud height instead.
impetus.options.extras.cloud_translucency.default=Default
impetus.options.extras.cloud_translucency.always=Always
impetus.options.extras.cloud_translucency.never=Never

impetus.options.extras.time.name=Time
impetus.options.extras.time.tooltip=Holds the world clock in day or night.\n\nSingle-player creative worlds only. The clock keeps running; it is pushed back inside the chosen half whenever it leaves it, rather than being frozen.
impetus.options.extras.time.default=Default
impetus.options.extras.time.day=Day Only
impetus.options.extras.time.night=Night Only
impetus.options.extras.weather.name=Weather
impetus.options.extras.weather.tooltip=Holds the weather.\n\nSingle-player creative worlds only. Unlike Rain & Snow this changes the weather itself, so mob spawning, crop growth and sounds follow it.
impetus.options.extras.weather.default=Default
impetus.options.extras.weather.clear=Clear
impetus.options.extras.weather.rain=Rain
impetus.options.extras.weather.thunder=Thunder

impetus.options.extras.render.item_frames.name=Item Frames
impetus.options.extras.render.item_frames.tooltip=Draws item frames and their contents.\n\nA framed item is a full item model, so a wall of them in a storage room costs about as much as the same number of dropped items.
impetus.options.extras.render.item_frame_lod.name=Item Frame LOD
impetus.options.extras.render.item_frame_lod.tooltip=Distance past which a framed item drops the faces the frame hides anyway, and a framed map is skipped entirely.\n\nA backport of MoreCulling's Frame LOD. Only the four side faces of a 3D model go; flat sprite items are untouched, so the visible difference at distance is nothing.
impetus.options.extras.render.item_frame_name_tag.name=Item Frame Name Tags
impetus.options.extras.render.item_frame_name_tag.tooltip=Draws the hover name of a named item in a frame.
impetus.options.extras.render.armor_stands.name=Armor Stands
impetus.options.extras.render.armor_stands.tooltip=Draws armor stands.\n\nA hidden armor stand keeps its name tag, so stands used as signposts still work.
impetus.options.extras.render.paintings.name=Paintings
impetus.options.extras.render.paintings.tooltip=Draws paintings.
impetus.options.extras.render.player_name_tag.name=Player Name Tags
impetus.options.extras.render.player_name_tag.tooltip=Draws the name above other players.
impetus.options.extras.render.dropped_items.name=Fancy Dropped Items
impetus.options.extras.render.dropped_items.tooltip=Draws a pile of dropped items as up to five stacked models, scaled by stack size.\n\nOff draws one model per pile whatever the stack size. Worth it on a floor covered in drops: a single stack of 64 otherwise costs five full item models every frame.
impetus.options.extras.render.beacons.name=Beacon Beams
impetus.options.extras.render.beacons.tooltip=Draws beacon beams.
impetus.options.extras.render.limit_beacon_beam.name=Limit Beacon Beam Height
impetus.options.extras.render.limit_beacon_beam.tooltip=Stops beacon beams at the world ceiling instead of letting the last segment draw past it.
impetus.options.extras.render.enchanting_books.name=Enchanting Table Books
impetus.options.extras.render.enchanting_books.tooltip=Draws the animated floating book above an enchanting table.
impetus.options.extras.render.pistons.name=Moving Pistons
impetus.options.extras.render.pistons.tooltip=Draws the extending piston head and the block it pushes.\n\nOff makes pushed blocks jump to their destination rather than sliding. The piston still works; only the in-between animation is skipped. Worth it beside a large redstone contraption.

impetus.options.extras.budget.enabled.name=Render Budget
impetus.options.extras.budget.enabled.tooltip=Adaptive client-side render budgeting for weaker machines, after GpuShift.\n\nMeasures frame time against a target and, when the game falls behind, skips drawing distant idle mobs and thins cosmetic particles. Players, named, glowing and mounted mobs, anything within 32 blocks, weather and block particles are never touched.\n\nIt only budgets what is expensive in the scene — many far mobs or many particles. If the overlay's skipped counters sit at zero, there is nothing here for it to save, and a server, shader or chunk-loading bottleneck will not move at all.
impetus.options.extras.budget.profile.name=Budget Profile
impetus.options.extras.budget.profile.tooltip=How hard the budget pushes.\n\nQuality targets 45 FPS and keeps at least 85%% of particles and 128 blocks of mobs. Balanced targets 60 FPS with the values below as set. Performance targets ~71 FPS, caps particles at 45%% and mobs at 72 blocks, and skips distant mobs even when not over target.
impetus.options.extras.budget_profile.quality=Quality
impetus.options.extras.budget_profile.balanced=Balanced
impetus.options.extras.budget_profile.performance=Performance
impetus.options.extras.budget.adaptive.name=Adaptive
impetus.options.extras.budget.adaptive.tooltip=Tightens the budget one further step while frames run 20%% or more over the profile target: particles drop another 20 points (never below 25%%) and the mob distance shortens by 16 blocks (never below 48). Relaxes again on its own.
impetus.options.extras.budget.particles.name=Particle Budget
impetus.options.extras.budget.particles.tooltip=The share of cosmetic particles allowed to spawn, spread evenly so no burst is dropped whole.\n\n70%% keeps things pretty, 65%% is the balanced default, 55%% suits heavy packs. Off leaves particles alone entirely — pick that if another mod already manages them.
impetus.options.extras.budget.entities.name=Skip Distant Mobs
impetus.options.extras.budget.entities.tooltip=Stops drawing non-player living entities beyond the distance below while over target.\n\nA mob must sit past the line for six consecutive frames before it goes and comes back 12 blocks inside it, so nothing flickers at the edge. Its shadow goes with it.
impetus.options.extras.budget.entity_distance.name=Mob Distance
impetus.options.extras.budget.entity_distance.tooltip=Blocks past which idle mobs may be skipped. The profile clamps this: Quality never below 128, Performance never above 72, and an active shader pack never below 64.
impetus.options.extras.budget.block_entities.name=Skip Distant Block Entities
impetus.options.extras.budget.block_entities.tooltip=Stops drawing vanilla's decorative block entity renderers (chests, ender chests, signs, heads, banners, beds, shulker boxes, enchanting table books) beyond the distance below while over target.\n\nThe one under your crosshair, anything modded, and the functional ones (beacons, spawners, pistons, portals) always draw. The block itself stays; only the animated model on top goes, so a far chest becomes a plain box rather than a hole.
impetus.options.extras.budget.block_entity_distance.name=Block Entity Distance
impetus.options.extras.budget.block_entity_distance.tooltip=Blocks past which decorative block entities and item frames may be skipped. Vanilla already stops drawing most of them at 64, so the useful settings sit below that; a shader pack adds 32.
impetus.options.extras.budget.item_frames.name=Skip Distant Item Frames
impetus.options.extras.budget.item_frames.tooltip=Stops drawing plain item frames beyond the block entity distance while over target. Frames holding a map, glowing frames and named frames are kept, since those are the ones read from afar.
impetus.options.extras.budget.overlay.name=Budget Overlay
impetus.options.extras.budget.overlay.tooltip=Adds the budget's frame-time reading, current limits and last-tick skipped counts to the HUD, in the same corner as the FPS readout. F8 toggles it in game.\n\nThe quickest way to tell whether the budget is doing anything: if the skipped counts stay at zero, this scene has nothing for it to save.

impetus.options.extras.booster.enabled.name=GPU Booster
impetus.options.extras.booster.enabled.tooltip=The companion to the render budget, after Mr.Toad's GPUBooster: where the budget decides what not to draw, this makes what is drawn cheaper to hand over.\n\nOf the original's set, the parts with something left to do on 1.12.2 are here. Direct state access, buffer pooling and a depth renderbuffer need nothing: this renderer already owns immutable buffer storage and mapped uploads, and vanilla 1.12.2 already keeps depth in a renderbuffer.
impetus.options.extras.booster.fast_random.name=Fast Random
impetus.options.extras.booster.fast_random.tooltip=Gives every entity and particle a cheaper random generator.\n\nVanilla builds a full java.util.Random per entity and per particle, each costing an atomic, a seed race and a clock read, and serves bell-curve numbers through a locked loop. This one is two words of state seeded from a counter, with a table-driven normal. Most visible in particle-heavy scenes, which is exactly where the particle budget works. Existing entities keep their generator until they reload.
impetus.options.extras.booster.fast_math.name=Fast Math
impetus.options.extras.booster.fast_math.tooltip=Cheaper angle wrapping and log2 in MathHelper.\n\nWrapping an angle into ±180 is done for every rendered entity several times a frame, and vanilla does it with a floating-point remainder that the JVM cannot inline. This uses a divide and a floor instead, with identical results for any angle a mob can carry. The sine table is not here because Equilibrium's compact table already covers it.
impetus.options.extras.booster.stream_uploads.name=Streamed Vertex Uploads
impetus.options.extras.booster.stream_uploads.tooltip=Draws GUI, item, entity, particle and block entity geometry through one streamed vertex buffer instead of client-side arrays.\n\nVanilla hands the driver a pointer into Java memory for every Tessellator draw, which the driver must copy before it can return, and which some drivers route through a slow compatibility path. On OpenGL 4.4 hardware the bytes go straight into a persistently mapped ring with no driver call in between; older hardware gets the classic orphaned streaming buffer. Gains depend on the driver; if anything draws wrong after enabling it, this is the switch to turn off first.

impetus.options.extras.parallel.enabled.name=Parallel Ticking
impetus.options.extras.parallel.enabled.tooltip=Ticks the integrated server's entities across a pool of worker threads instead of one, after AxalotL's Async (in turn from MCMT).\n\nEntities are grouped by 4x4-chunk region so neighbours stay on one thread, and the world's shared tables (block writes, entity lists, chunk map, scheduled ticks) are guarded so the threads cannot corrupt them. Players, projectiles, falling blocks, boats, shulkers and the dragon always tick on the main thread; TNT, drops, orbs and hopper carts do too by default (parallel_ticking.synchronizedEntities in impetus-extras.cfg).\n\nExperimental: a mob's behaviour can differ subtly when its neighbours tick at the same moment, and a mod that assumes one thread may break. Needs a relaunch to take effect.
impetus.options.extras.parallel.threads.name=Worker Threads
impetus.options.extras.parallel.threads.tooltip=How many threads tick entities. Auto uses about three quarters of the machine's cores, leaving one for the render thread; more than the core count only adds contention. Applies to the next batch.
impetus.options.extras.parallel.entities.name=Entities
impetus.options.extras.parallel.entities.tooltip=Ticks vanilla entities in parallel. This is where the time goes on a busy server: mob farms, villages, animal pens, item-heavy machines. Off leaves the pool idle while the other switches still apply.
impetus.options.extras.parallel.modded_entities.name=Modded Entities
impetus.options.extras.parallel.modded_entities.tooltip=Also ticks entities registered by other mods in parallel. Off (the default) keeps every modded entity on the main thread, since their code has never been asked to share the world with another thread. Try it on a pack you can afford to crash; add any offender to parallel_ticking.synchronizedEntities as modid:name or modid:* to exempt it.
impetus.options.extras.parallel.random_ticks.name=Random Ticks
impetus.options.extras.parallel.random_ticks.tooltip=Runs block random ticks (crop growth, fire, leaf decay, grass spread) through the pool. The positions are still chosen in vanilla's order on the main thread; only each block's reaction is spread out, and every block write still funnels through the world lock.
impetus.options.extras.parallel.spawning.name=Mob Spawning
impetus.options.extras.parallel.spawning.tooltip=Evaluates natural spawn attempts one chunk per task. The per-type caps and eligible chunk sweep stay serial and identical to vanilla; the placement search, light and collision checks run in parallel, and each spawn registers through the world lock.

impetus.options.extras.baked.enabled.name=Baked Block Entities
impetus.options.extras.baked.enabled.tooltip=Draws resting chests, ender chests, signs, beds and shulker boxes as part of the terrain mesh instead of through their block entity renderers, after FoundationGames' Enhanced Block Entities and the Better Block Entities design.\n\nA block entity renderer rebuilds and submits its model every frame; the mesh draws it once per chunk rebuild. The geometry is baked from the renderer's own model, so nothing looks different, except that it now gets smooth lighting and is visible from any distance. A chest that opens hands itself back to its renderer for the animation and returns to the mesh when it closes. Sign text is still drawn by the renderer.
impetus.options.extras.baked.chests.name=Chests
impetus.options.extras.baked.chests.tooltip=Chests and trapped chests, single and double. Both halves of a double chest are baked from the large model and split at the seam, so section culling stays exact.
impetus.options.extras.baked.ender_chests.name=Ender Chests
impetus.options.extras.baked.ender_chests.tooltip=Ender chests, which share the chest model under their own texture.
impetus.options.extras.baked.signs.name=Signs
impetus.options.extras.baked.signs.tooltip=Sign boards and posts, standing and wall-mounted. The text is still drawn by the renderer over the baked board, since it needs the font renderer either way.
impetus.options.extras.baked.beds.name=Beds
impetus.options.extras.baked.beds.tooltip=Beds in every colour. Beds never animate, so the renderer is skipped entirely for a placed bed.
impetus.options.extras.baked.shulker_boxes.name=Shulker Boxes
impetus.options.extras.baked.shulker_boxes.tooltip=Closed shulker boxes in every colour and facing. A box that opens hands itself back to its renderer until it closes again.

impetus.options.extras.network.large_packets.name=Large Packets
impetus.options.extras.network.large_packets.tooltip=Lifts the wire caps a large pack outgrows, after TonimatasDEV's Packet Fixer: the 21-bit frame length (2 MB per packet), the 2 MB compressed packet and NBT limits, 32 KB strings, the 1 MB clientbound and 32 KB serverbound mod payloads, and the 2 MB chunk data cap.\n\nThese are the "Badly compressed packet", "Tried to read NBT tag that was too big", "Payload may not be larger than" and "Chunk Packet trying to allocate too much memory" crashes. The integrated server and the client both carry the change; a remote server needs its own fix to send anything larger than vanilla allows.
impetus.options.extras.network.read_timeout.name=Read Timeout
impetus.options.extras.network.read_timeout.tooltip=Seconds a connection may go silent before either side drops it. Vanilla's 30 is easy to miss while a heavy pack finishes joining or the world is generating.
impetus.options.extras.network.login_timeout.name=Login Timeout
impetus.options.extras.network.login_timeout.tooltip=Seconds the server gives a client to finish logging in before "Took too long to log in". Vanilla's 30 is the time to receive and reconcile the registries of every mod.
impetus.options.extras.network.keep_alive_timeout.name=Keep-Alive Timeout
impetus.options.extras.network.keep_alive_timeout.tooltip=Seconds a client may take to answer a keep-alive before "Timed out". Vanilla's 15 is missed by a long client-side stall such as a large chunk batch or a garbage collection pause.

impetus.options.extras.threads.render_priority.name=Render Thread Priority
impetus.options.extras.threads.render_priority.tooltip=Java scheduling priority of the client thread, which runs rendering, input and sound, after wisecase2's StutterFix. Normal (5) is what the game uses; raising it asks the operating system to prefer the frame over background work such as chunk building and the integrated server. StutterFix's own default is 10 on machines with more than four cores. How much the OS honours it varies, and on Linux it is ignored unless the JVM runs with -XX:ThreadPriorityPolicy=1.
impetus.options.extras.threads.server_priority.name=Server Thread Priority
impetus.options.extras.threads.server_priority.tooltip=Java scheduling priority of the integrated server thread. Normal (5) is vanilla; StutterFix suggests 8, above the chunk builders and below the render thread, so ticks keep up under load without stealing frames.
impetus.options.extras.threads.chunk_builder_priority.name=Chunk Builder Priority
impetus.options.extras.threads.chunk_builder_priority.tooltip=Java scheduling priority of the chunk mesh builder threads. Impetus starts them at 3; lowering to 1 keeps chunk loading from ever contending with the frame at the cost of slower loading, which is StutterFix's "priority cut-off" for the lower half of its workers. Applies when the renderer next reloads.
impetus.options.extras.threads.remove_yield.name=Remove Render Yield
impetus.options.extras.threads.remove_yield.tooltip=Skips the Thread.yield() the render loop makes once per frame. Yielding offers the core to any other runnable thread, which on a loaded machine hands it to a worker and delays the next frame; StutterFix reports steadier frame times without it. Off by default since some drivers and mods rely on the pause.

impetus.quick_setup.title=Impetus Quick Setup
impetus.quick_setup.body=Impetus can trade a little distant detail for frame time on weaker machines. Pick a preset to turn on the Render Budget and GPU Booster together, or leave both off. Everything here can be changed later under Video Settings > Extras, or by pressing F9 in game. This screen only appears once.
impetus.quick_setup.balanced=Balanced
impetus.quick_setup.balanced.help=Recommended: skips distant mobs and thins cosmetic particles only while frames run over 60 FPS's budget.
impetus.quick_setup.performance=Performance
impetus.quick_setup.performance.help=For weak machines: always skips distant mobs and keeps particles at 45%%, aiming past 70 FPS.
impetus.quick_setup.quality=Quality
impetus.quick_setup.quality.help=Gentle: only steps in below 45 FPS and never drops below 85%% of particles or 128 blocks of mobs.
impetus.quick_setup.settings=Open Settings
impetus.quick_setup.settings.help=Opens the Extras page to set everything by hand.
impetus.quick_setup.skip=Not Now
impetus.quick_setup.skip.help=Leaves both features off; they stay available under Extras.

impetus.key.category=Impetus
impetus.key.budget_overlay=Toggle Budget Overlay
impetus.key.open_settings=Open Impetus Settings

impetus.options.extras.overlay.fps.name=Show FPS
impetus.options.extras.overlay.fps.tooltip=Shows the frame rate on the HUD.
impetus.options.extras.overlay.fps_extended.name=Extended FPS
impetus.options.extras.overlay.fps_extended.tooltip=Adds the five-second average and the 1%% and 0.1%% lows.\n\nThe lows are the mean of the slowest 1%% and 0.1%% of frames — the benchmarking definition — so they measure stutter rather than throughput. A high average with poor lows is a hitching game.
impetus.options.extras.overlay.coords.name=Show Coordinates
impetus.options.extras.overlay.coords.tooltip=Shows your position on the HUD.
impetus.options.extras.overlay.ignore_reduced_debug_info.name=Ignore Reduced Debug Info
impetus.options.extras.overlay.ignore_reduced_debug_info.tooltip=Shows coordinates even where the server has asked that they be hidden.\n\nServers set that rule deliberately, usually on adventure maps or minigames. Overriding it is not a client-side exploit — the coordinates were already in the client — but it is the sort of thing a server may consider one.
impetus.options.extras.overlay.corner.name=Overlay Corner
impetus.options.extras.overlay.corner.tooltip=Which corner the FPS and coordinate lines are anchored to.
impetus.options.extras.overlay_corner.top_left=Top Left
impetus.options.extras.overlay_corner.top_right=Top Right
impetus.options.extras.overlay_corner.bottom_left=Bottom Left
impetus.options.extras.overlay_corner.bottom_right=Bottom Right
impetus.options.extras.overlay.text_contrast.name=Text Contrast
impetus.options.extras.overlay.text_contrast.tooltip=How overlay text is made readable against the world behind it.
impetus.options.extras.text_contrast.none=None
impetus.options.extras.text_contrast.background=Background
impetus.options.extras.text_contrast.shadow=Shadow

impetus.options.extras.toasts.all.name=Toasts
impetus.options.extras.toasts.all.tooltip=Master switch for toast pop-ups.\n\nSuppressed toasts are dropped when they are raised, so they never queue up behind the ones you did want.
impetus.options.extras.toasts.advancement.name=Advancement Toasts
impetus.options.extras.toasts.advancement.tooltip=Shows the pop-up when an advancement is earned.
impetus.options.extras.toasts.recipe.name=Recipe Toasts
impetus.options.extras.toasts.recipe.tooltip=Shows the pop-up when a recipe is unlocked. The usual reason to be here: a large modpack unlocks hundreds at once.
impetus.options.extras.toasts.tutorial.name=Tutorial Toasts
impetus.options.extras.toasts.tutorial.tooltip=Shows the tutorial hints given to new players.
impetus.options.extras.toasts.system.name=System Toasts
impetus.options.extras.toasts.system.tooltip=Shows system messages such as world-save warnings.

impetus.options.extras.vertical_sync.name=Vertical Sync
impetus.options.extras.vertical_sync.tooltip=How frames are presented to the display.\n\nOn caps the frame rate to the refresh rate and removes tearing, at the cost of a dropped frame costing a whole refresh period. Adaptive keeps that behaviour above the refresh rate but disengages below it, so a dropped frame costs one frame rather than half a refresh.\n\nAdaptive is only offered where the driver supports tear control.
impetus.options.extras.vertical_sync.adaptive=Adaptive

impetus.options.extras.steady_debug_hud.name=Steady Debug HUD
impetus.options.extras.steady_debug_hud.tooltip=Rebuilds the F3 overlay text on a fixed interval rather than every frame.\n\nCheaper — some of those lines hit the world — and more readable: at 200 FPS the vanilla overlay changes faster than it can be read.
impetus.options.extras.steady_debug_hud_refresh.name=Debug HUD Interval
impetus.options.extras.steady_debug_hud_refresh.tooltip=How often the F3 overlay text is rebuilt.

impetus.options.extras.panini.name=Panini Projection
impetus.options.extras.panini.tooltip=Applies a Panini projection to the finished frame.\n\nPanini is cylindrical horizontally and rectilinear vertically, which is what lets a wide field of view avoid the corner stretching a plain perspective projection gives past about 100 degrees.\n\nIt redistributes what was already drawn rather than widening the view, and is disabled while a shader pack is loaded — a pack owns the frame and its own screen-space effects would fall out of register with it.
impetus.options.extras.panini_strength.name=Panini Strength
impetus.options.extras.panini_strength.tooltip=How much of the Panini remap to apply. Zero is off.

impetus.options.extras.advanced_item_tooltips.name=Advanced Item Tooltips
impetus.options.extras.advanced_item_tooltips.tooltip=Shows an item's registry name and durability in its tooltip.\n\nThe same setting as F3 + H, bound to the vanilla option rather than mirrored, so the two can never disagree.
impetus.options.extras.mod_name_tooltip.name=Mod Name Tooltip
impetus.options.extras.mod_name_tooltip.tooltip=Appends the source mod's name to item tooltips.\n\nSkipped when JEI has already added the same line.
impetus.options.extras.light_updates.name=Light Updates
impetus.options.extras.light_updates.tooltip=Recomputes lighting on the client.\n\nOff is a large saving and a large caveat: light stops updating visibly, so a torch placed afterwards lights nothing until the chunk is rebuilt for some other reason. A warning is drawn on the HUD while this is off, because the symptom otherwise looks exactly like a rendering bug.
impetus.options.extras.prevent_shaders.name=Prevent Vanilla Shaders
impetus.options.extras.prevent_shaders.tooltip=Blocks the vanilla post-processing effect chain — the spectator, creeper and entity-view shaders.\n\nNothing to do with shader packs; Umbra is unaffected. This is for the effects that are easy to activate by accident.
impetus.options.extras.profile_entity_rendering.name=Profile Entity Rendering
impetus.options.extras.profile_entity_rendering.tooltip=Splits entity and block-entity rendering by type in the F3 profiler pie chart.\n\nVanilla reports all of it as one slice, which tells you entities are expensive but not which ones. This is what turns an unexplained frame-time cliff into a mod name.
impetus.options.extras.autosave_interval.name=Autosave Interval
impetus.options.extras.autosave_interval.tooltip=How often the single-player world is written to disk. Vanilla saves every 45 seconds.\n\nA longer interval means fewer stutters and more progress lost to an unclean exit. Off disables the periodic save entirely; the world is still saved on quit.

# ==========================================================================================
# Dynamic Lights
# ==========================================================================================
impetus.options.pages.dynamiclights=Dynamic Lights

impetus.options.dynamiclights.mode.name=Dynamic Lights
impetus.options.dynamiclights.mode.tooltip=Lets held and dropped items, burning mobs and other entities light the world around them. Higher settings update the lighting more often as sources move; Realtime updates every tick. The cost is rebuilding the chunk mesh around each moving light, so this scales with how many lit things are near you rather than with render distance.
impetus.options.dynamiclights.mode.off=Off
impetus.options.dynamiclights.mode.slow=Slow
impetus.options.dynamiclights.mode.fast=Fast
impetus.options.dynamiclights.mode.realtime=Realtime

impetus.options.dynamiclights.self.name=Handheld Lighting
impetus.options.dynamiclights.self.tooltip=Lets what you are holding or wearing light your own surroundings. Worth turning off with a shader pack that does its own handheld lighting, otherwise the two stack and everything nearby is washed out.
impetus.options.dynamiclights.entities.name=Entity Lighting
impetus.options.dynamiclights.entities.tooltip=Lets other entities light the world: mobs carrying torches, dropped glowstone, item frames, burning creepers. Your own light is governed separately by Handheld Lighting.
impetus.options.dynamiclights.block_entities.name=Block Entity Lighting
impetus.options.dynamiclights.block_entities.tooltip=Lets block entities light the world. Nothing in vanilla uses this — light-emitting blocks already have real block light — so it only does anything with a mod whose machines register a dynamic light handler.
impetus.options.dynamiclights.water_sensitive.name=Extinguish Underwater
impetus.options.dynamiclights.water_sensitive.tooltip=Stops water-sensitive sources glowing while submerged. A torch or a lava bucket goes dark underwater; a nether star does not.
impetus.options.dynamiclights.debug_info.name=Show Debug Info
impetus.options.dynamiclights.debug_info.tooltip=Adds a line to the F3 overlay counting the tracked light sources, and how many of them re-lit their chunks on the last update.

impetus.options.dynamiclights.tnt.name=TNT Lighting
impetus.options.dynamiclights.tnt.tooltip=How primed TNT glows as its fuse burns down. Simple holds one brightness for the whole fuse; Fancy ramps up towards the explosion, which looks right but re-lights the surrounding chunks each time the value changes.
impetus.options.dynamiclights.creeper.name=Creeper Lighting
impetus.options.dynamiclights.creeper.tooltip=How a creeper glows while it is flashing. Simple holds one brightness; Fancy follows the flash, which re-lights the surrounding chunks as it swells.
impetus.options.dynamiclights.explosive.off=Off
impetus.options.dynamiclights.explosive.simple=Simple
impetus.options.dynamiclights.explosive.fancy=Fancy
